.TH STYXSEG 1
.SH NAME
styxseg \- does a Styx message survive being chopped into radio-sized pieces
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B styxseg
[
.B -d
] [
.B -b
.I budget
] [
.B -n
.I reads
] [
.B -m
.I mountpoint
] [
.B -l
.I lo
] [
.B -h
.I hi
] [
.B -s
.I stride
] [
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Styxseg
makes two pipes and two chopping processes, which together are a
bidirectional link with a write limit on it,
serves one end of it with
.IR exportfs ,
mounts the other with
.IR devmnt ,
and then reads a file through the mount and compares what comes back.
.PP
It exists to answer one question without a board.
.IR armv7mstyx (8)
mounts a namespace over an AX.25 conversation whose information field is
109 bytes, which is below the 256
.B /os/inferno/port/devmnt.c
insists on, so a pipe sits between Styx and the radio and two processes
chop writes into pieces the frame holds.  That worked for a message
inside one frame, and then a 284-byte Rread \- the 273 bytes of
.B #P/arch
and its eleven-byte header \- came back 273 bytes the first time and 271
the second.  Four things in that path could lose a pair of bytes: the
relay, 
.IR devmnt 's
reassembly,
.IR devax25 's
connected mode, and the air.
.B This takes the radio out and leaves everything else.
.PP
It is not a model of the radio and does not pretend to be.  A pipe is
ordered, reliable and never drops a frame, which is exactly what the
connected mode is supposed to provide and what this therefore holds
fixed.  A pass here says the chopping and the reassembly are sound; it
says nothing about the device or the link.
.PP
With no
.I file
argument it writes its own, of each size from
.I lo
to
.I hi
by
.IR stride ,
and reads each back
.I reads
times.  The content is a counted pattern rather than a constant byte, so
a piece arriving out of order or a boundary dropped shows up as a
difference at a byte whose value says which piece it came from.
.PP
The options are
.TP
.B -b \fIbudget\fP
The write limit in the middle of the link, 109 by default, which is the
conversation's information field: the CC1120 carries 125 bytes and
.IR ax25 (3)
takes sixteen for the address field.
.TP
.B -n \fIreads\fP
How many times to read each file, two by default.  More than one
because a transfer that loses bytes may do it on the second message
rather than the first, which is exactly what the bench saw.
.TP
.B -l \fIlo\fP, -h \fIhi\fP, -s \fIstride\fP
The sizes to sweep when no file is named.  A sweep is what finds a size
that fails, rather than confirming one that does not.
.TP
.B -m \fImountpoint\fP
Where to mount the served end;
.B /n/client
by default, because it exists in every root this has to run in.
.TP
.B -d
Print every transfer in each direction.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/styxseg.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR radiostyx (1),
.IR ax25 (3),
.IR mount (1),
.IR export (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Prints
.B STYXSEG-OK
and exits zero if every read matched, and
.BR STYXSEG-SHORT ,
.B STYXSEG-DIFFER
or
.B STYXSEG-FAILED
with the size and the byte otherwise.
.SH BUGS
.B -M
will negotiate a message size with
.IR sys-fversion (2)
before the mount, and then
.I mount
sends its own Tversion on the same connection, which 9P does not allow
on an established one: both ends wait and nothing is printed.  It
defaults to off and is kept only so that nobody rediscovers that it
hangs by construction.  Reaching a particular message size properly
wants the transport to advertise a small iounit, because that is what
.I devmnt
clamps to.
.PP
A budget below about ten makes the sweep slow enough to matter: a
284-byte message becomes 284 separate writes and each is a process
switch.
